1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,900
Support teams handle hundreds of
incidents every day.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,700
Most were solved before.
The knowledge just isn't connected.

3
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:10,900
So the same fault gets
diagnosed twice.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,600
One hundred and four records,
from four real systems.

5
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,200
The service desk. SAP.
SharePoint. And the knowledge base.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:23,900
Three checks per record.
Restricted data never indexed.

7
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,300
Now Find a Solution.
You type the problem.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,700
Matches rank by how well
they've worked, not just wording.

9
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:37,700
Add the incident detail. Error code,
module, and environment.

10
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:41,700
Three badges light up.
It matched this ticket exactly.

11
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:45,900
Now the interesting part.
Same code, test environment.

12
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,900
A different root cause.
That's the trap it avoids.

13
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,100
The same search crosses languages.
One question, three languages.

14
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,900
English, Malay, Chinese,
and now Japanese.

15
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,300
Translate the Chinese one
and it's the same fix.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:08,500
So any team's knowledge
stays reusable across the company.

17
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,500
Knowledge from any team,
in any language.

18
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:15,700
This is the vocabulary
a consultant actually uses.

19
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:20,300
Production order won't create.
Twelve out of twelve worked.

20
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:24,300
Now the same category,
a record that failed before.

21
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,900
Seven of nine is too low.
So it declines.

22
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,900
Same category, different
record history.

23
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:35,300
Chat is the second way in.

24
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:39,500
I forgot my password.
It answers directly.

25
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:43,700
Worked forty-five out of forty-five.

26
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:48,000
Now something vaguer.
A finance user cannot open it.

27
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,000
It refuses to commit.
The check is not satisfied.

28
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:56,000
Notice what it doesn't do.
No numbers, no sources.

29
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,400
Then I add what I know.
The code, the module, production.

30
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:04,600
Now it commits.
Same question, better context.

31
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,200
Nothing changed but the
evidence I gave it.

32
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:12,300
What if the fix doesn't work?

33
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:16,700
I tell it the email fix failed.
It rules it out.

34
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:21,300
Then it offers a different fix.
Large attachments.

35
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:25,700
A real conversation,
not a search box.

36
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:30,500
It remembers what already failed,
so it doesn't offer it again.

37
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:35,400
And this isn't only
for troubleshooting.

38
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:39,800
I ask for a new starter's
laptop procedure.

39
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,200
It finds the official standard
procedure document.

40
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,100
Finally, something it
should not answer.

41
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:53,300
I run out of milk.
It says no match exists.

42
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:57,300
That refusal is the feature.

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,200
Two ways in. Both transparent.

44
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,700
Both say when they don't know.
